## The task: HiMCM 2002 *School Busing*
Built a full, reproducible modeling pipeline in `code/`:

- **`sbrp.py`** — the model: a **capacitated VRP with time windows (CVRPTW)** solved by randomized Clarke–Wright savings + 2-opt route pools, then an **exact set-partitioning MILP** (PuLP/CBC). A single vehicle-reuse coupling captures the crux: `#buses = n_E + n_H − min(n_E, n_H_short)`, i.e. buses that drop elementary kids can immediately run a high-school route if it fits the **stagger** window.
- **`run_experiments.py`** — rural/urban base cases + 6 sensitivity sweeps.
- **`validate_small.py`** — exhaustive exact-optimum cross-check.
- **`testing.py`** — equity (α) frontier, Monte-Carlo robustness, safety-margin study.
- **`sens_var.py`** — variable-cost sensitivity.

### Headline results
| | Separate (S) | Combined/staggered (C) |
|---|---|---|
| Rural (596 students) | 10 buses · **$1,351/day** | 7 buses · **$1,010/day** (25% saving) |
| Urban | 13 buses · $1,673 | 9 buses · $1,201 (28%) |

- **Validation:** our solver is within **0.61%** of exact optimum (single school) and matches it exactly (0.00%) on the two-school pool.
- **Key lever:** a stagger **Δ ≥ ~30 min** is required; savings saturate by Δ ≈ 40–50 min.
- **Equity:** combined routing *also* shortens high-school rides and cuts student-minutes — better on both axes with a small α weight.
- **Pre-implementation must-fix:** designing at the 60-min limit breaks the one-hour rule ~35% of the time; a **≤45-min nominal design** drops that to ~1%.
- Robust across all cost parameters (still wins at $2.00/km).

## Deliverable
`results/solution_report.md` — text-only, UTF-8, no images/base64, ~4,000 words, with equations, tables, and the required **school-board article**. Verified it exists and is internally consistent with `results/experiments.json`. All work committed to git.

Note: this run was autonomous (no user present), so the bootstrap identity is a placeholder you can override anytime.
